sections.s2_technical:
The FOUR remaining differences after Java 8:

```java
// ═══ DIFFERENCE 1: CONSTRUCTORS — abstract class YES, interface NO ═══
public abstract class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    protected BigDecimal balance;

    protected Account(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
        // Constructor initializes shared state for ALL subclasses
        if (accountId == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("required");
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.balance = balance;
    }
}
// Interface: NO constructor. Can't initialize state on creation.

// ═══ DIFFERENCE 2: INSTANCE FIELDS — abstract class YES, interface NO ═══
public abstract class Account {
    protected BigDecimal balance;           // mutable instance field ✅
    private final String accountId;         // final instance field ✅
    private List<String> auditLog;          // private field ✅
}

public interface Auditable {
    BigDecimal MAX_AMOUNT = new BigDecimal("10000000");  // public static final ONLY!
    // BigDecimal balance;  → COMPILE ERROR! Can't have instance fields!
}

// ═══ DIFFERENCE 3: ACCESS MODIFIERS — abstract class ANY, interface public only ═══
public abstract class Account {
    protected void internalValidate() { }   // protected ✅
    private void audit() { }                // private ✅
    void packageMethod() { }                // default access ✅
    public void deposit() { }              // public ✅
}

public interface Auditable {
    void generateReport();                  // implicitly public
    default void logAudit() { }             // implicitly public
    private void helper() { }              // private (Java 9+) ✅
    // protected void internal() { }        // ❌ COMPILE ERROR!
}

// ═══ DIFFERENCE 4: SINGLE vs MULTIPLE INHERITANCE ═══
class SavingsAccount extends Account implements Auditable, Notifiable, Taxable {
    // Extends ONE abstract class. Implements MANY interfaces.
}
// class X extends Account, AnotherClass { }  → ❌ COMPILE ERROR!
```

The decision flowchart:

```java
// STEP 1: Do you need instance fields (mutable state shared across subclasses)?
//   YES → abstract class (interface can't have fields)
//   NO → continue

// STEP 2: Do you need a constructor to initialize state?
//   YES → abstract class (interface can't have constructors)
//   NO → continue

// STEP 3: Do multiple UNRELATED classes need this capability?
//   YES → interface (multiple inheritance of type)
//   NO → continue

// STEP 4: Is this a family of RELATED classes (IS-A hierarchy)?
//   YES → abstract class (type hierarchy with shared behavior)
//   NO → interface (default choice for contracts)

// RULE: when in doubt → interface. Use abstract class ONLY when you NEED
// constructors or shared mutable state.
```

Real-world Spring examples:

```java
// Spring uses INTERFACES for contracts:
public interface JpaRepository<T, ID> { }  // ANY entity can implement
public interface Serializable { }           // ANY class can implement

// Spring uses ABSTRACT CLASSES for shared state/template:
public abstract class AbstractController {
    private WebApplicationContext context;  // STATE! Interface can't have this.
    protected abstract ModelAndView handleRequest();  // template method
}

// Your banking code:
// Account (abstract class): has balance, accountId, constructor, protected methods
// Auditable (interface): capability — any class can be auditable
// Taxable (interface): capability — any class can be taxable
```

